  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nA 559 mb low currently over Southern Nevada continues to bring the \nmuch cooler (and welcoming for some) weather pattern. Current \ntemperatures this afternoon are only in the 60s and 70s west of the \nmountains and in the deserts, with 40s and 50s across the mountains.\nScan your eyes across the sky because that may be the last time \nyou see any large concentrations of clouds this week. The area of \nlow pressure moves further to the east toward the Four Corners \nRegion by Wednesday as high pressure off the coast expands. This \nwill begin a larger warming trend. By Wednesday, temperatures will\nrise 5-10 degrees with highs mostly near average along and west \nof the mountains. Comfortable and below normal temperatures remain\nfor the deserts with highs in the 70s and 80s. The wind flow \nbehind this system will start to turn offshore, with portions of \nthe coastal slopes and Inland Empire seeing elevated Santa Ana \nwinds.\n\nAs the aforementioned low pressure ejects into the Great Plains, a \ncutoff low will detach from this as the ridge of high pressure off \nthe coast amplifies into the Pac NW. The pressure gradient between \nthese two systems will tighten, leading to increased Santa Ana winds \nacross SoCal. Winds will begin to strengthen, at first more \nnortherly and northeasterly on Thursday, favoring the San Bernardino \nMts and LA Basin region. The low meanders somewhere to our south \nFriday into even Saturday, which will favor more easterly winds, \nleading to higher winds in the Riverside/San Diego County mountains \nand passes. High Wind Watches have been posted for all mountain \nareas, the Inland Empire and San Diego Co Foothills. Winds will gust \nnear 50-65 MPH at times, locally higher along coastal slopes and the \nSan Gorgonio Pass. All areas are under this watch Thursday and \nFriday, with San Diego County under the watch until Saturday as \nuncertainty remains in how long the high winds last.\n\nSanta Ana winds often mean warm conditions west of the mountains! \nHigh temperatures Thursday through Saturday will be in the 80s and \n90s in these regions. The coastal areas and inland Orange County \nwill see the greatest warming with some spots to see highs in the \nmid 90s by Friday. NBM shows 30-50% chance that portions of inland \nOrange County see highs above 95, about 10-30% in the San Diego \nmetro. Low temperatures will also stay above 70 degrees in some \nspots. A Heat Advisory has been issued for these areas Thursday \nthrough Saturday for moderate to locally high heat risk impacts.\n\nModels show the low weakening this weekend into early next week, as \na trough begins to enter the Pacific NW. This will turn winds \nonshore and bring cooling to the region. Models are in fair \nagreement that the trough will make some headway down the California \ncoastline, which would drop temperatures below normal sometime \naround Tuesday. If this occurs, cool and breezy weather will \naccompany this, and even some precipitation by the middle of next \nweek.",
